Work queue (in this order, per Andres - new items get appended at the end unless he
says otherwise). Reordered 2026-08-08 by gain/effort/surface-area/code-practice -
see the note above each moved/merged item for what changed and why.

Bigger features needing research first (moderate-high surface area):

- Push delivery: found live 2026-08-08 (journalctl, chasing the quota-
  reset investigation) - two send failures today, "Size of payload must
  not be greater than 4078 octets" (the Web Push/VAPID hard limit).
  Almost certainly a blocked-prompt push (PushDeliveryService::
  check_and_send_pushes()), whose prompt_context can carry a full Bash
  command/diff, pushing the payload over the limit - a quota push's
  payload is tiny by comparison, unlikely to be the cause. Needs a real
  cap/truncation on whatever field is oversized before it's handed to
  the push payload builder (NotificationContentBuilder), not just at
  send time.
- Mode CHANGES (accept edits / plan / manual / auto) aren't currently
  visible as discrete events in the transcript at all -
  render_mode_toggle_html() only shows the CURRENT mode, there's no
  record of "mode switched from X to Y at this point." (Split off from
  the plan-recognition item, shipped 2026-08-07 - see TranscriptService::
  find_exit_plan_mode_tool_use_ids()/parse_transcript_line() for that
  half.) Research already done: the transcript JSONL DOES have a
  dedicated line for this - {"type":"permission-mode","permissionMode":
  "default"|"acceptEdits"|"plan"|"auto"} (default=manual in this app's
  own terminology) - but it's logged redundantly (239 occurrences in one
  real session, mostly repeats of the unchanged value, not one-per-real-
  transition) and has NO timestamp field. Surfacing genuine "switched
  from X to Y here" events needs tracking last-seen mode across
  consecutive lines while scanning (stateful), which doesn't fit
  parse_transcript_line()'s current per-line-stateless model - and is
  harder for backward pagination ("Load older messages") than forward
  polling, since "previous" flips meaning depending on walk direction.
  Worth deciding: full bidirectional support, or a smaller scope (e.g.
  only ever detected going forward from whenever a session was first
  opened, never backfilled into older history)?

Larger architecture / consolidation work (bigger surface area):

- Multi-socket tmux discovery: today the whole app is wired to exactly one
  tmux socket path (Config::tmux_socket(), defaulting to
  /tmp/tmux-1000/default - tmux's own standard default path, not an app
  convention, which is why a plain `tmux` command Andres types by hand
  lands on the same server the app manages). Every TmuxService call is
  scoped to that one path - if a session ends up on a DIFFERENT tmux
  server (a different explicit -S path, or a fresh server auto-spawned at
  the conventional path after the original one's socket became
  unreachable - see the 2026-08-08 incident where /tmp/tmux-1000's
  contents got deleted out from under a still-running server, orphaning
  its sessions with no clean recovery path found), the app has zero
  visibility into it. Fixing this properly needs: TmuxService enumerating
  every tmux socket under /tmp/tmux-$UID/ (not just "default") and
  querying each one, SidecarStore gaining a field recording which socket a
  session actually lives on, and the SessionStart hook recording that at
  adoption time (today it only records the session NAME via `tmux
  display-message -p '#S'`, not which socket it ran that query against).
  A real structural change, not a small addition - scope/design it
  properly before starting, don't bolt it onto an unrelated phase. (The
  related "a live-but-untracked session shows up in the archived list
  too" gap this used to note is resolved now that Resume and Take over
  both ship - see the unify-claude-sessions plan, phases 5-6 shipped
  2026-08-08 - either action works correctly regardless of which
  category a row actually falls into.)
- Research: now that this app has a PreToolUse hook (see the pending-tool
  full-command-preview feature), survey whether other Claude Code hook events
  (PostToolUse, Notification, UserPromptSubmit, etc) could replace/improve any
  of the pane-scraping this app currently does - e.g. catching a tool result
  right as it lands instead of waiting for the next transcript poll. Stop is
  already ruled out (tried 2026-08-02: Claude Code writes the transcript file
  ~1.7-2s BEFORE the Stop hook fires, the opposite of what you'd assume, so it
  has nothing to add over reading the transcript directly - verify actual
  hook firing order/latency against a real session before building on an
  assumption about it, same as this finding).

session.php transcript-rendering pass (three related items, same
templates/rendering code - Andres's own framing 2026-08-08, worth
scoping as one cohesive pass rather than three separate ones touching
BlockedPromptView/TranscriptView repeatedly):

- Tweak transcript entries to look more like the Claude app: user
  messages in bubbles; Claude's own messages free-flowing with no
  border/background; prompts and permissions stay boxed with their
  options, as today; subagents, plans, and tools can probably stay as
  they are now - boxed summary plus the existing expand/collapse
  toggles.
- A blocked prompt's context longer than a short "Do you want to
  proceed?"-style question (e.g. a real Bash command, a multi-line
  diff/explanation) should render as its own separate entry in the
  history, not inline inside the blocked-prompt card where it's harder to
  read. Today prompt_context always renders inline via the same
  render_collapsible_block() used for tool_use/tool_result blocks (see
  BlockedPromptView::blocked_prompt_rich_html(), ~line 169) - a collapsed
  one-liner inside the amber blocked-prompt card itself, never a
  standalone card in the transcript flow the way a real tool_use entry
  does. Needs a decision before implementing: the exact length/complexity
  threshold for "longer than a short question" (compare to
  collapsible_summary()'s existing TOOL_USE_SUMMARY_LINE_MAX=80 precedent
  - could reuse that same cutoff, or need its own), and what "its own
  entry" should actually look like (a real history-list entry styled like
  a tool_use block, vs. just a bigger/less-cramped card still inside the
  blocked-prompt section).
- A middle ground for tool calls/outputs between fully shown (spammy) and
  fully hidden (Andres loses enough context to trust generic narration
  like "as expected" / "the mistake was..." without seeing what was
  actually checked). Andres's own framing: "consider adding tool usage
  summaries when hidden, or a skill to enable better responses to tool
  outputs." Specific to this app's session pages (the existing "Show tool
  outputs" toggle - BlockedPromptView::render_collapsible_block()), not a
  general Claude Code behavior change.

Smaller polish (low effort, low risk):

- session.php: don't show the "new content" divider bar (markNewContent()
  in session.js) when there are no entries after it - presumably possible
  in some poll-timing edge case (e.g. the only "new" thing was a blocked-
  prompt/thinking-indicator update, not a real transcript entry).
- Add copy-to-clipboard icons to transcript entries and inner
  code/scrollable sections (e.g. the collapsible tool_use/tool_result
  blocks - BlockedPromptView::render_collapsible_block()/its session.js
  mirror) - copying currently means selecting text by hand.
- Two related nav/render polish items (Andres, 2026-08-08): (1) detect
  the iOS swipe-back gesture going from session.php to the dashboard and
  show a loading blanket over the page during that transition, same
  spirit as a real native-app transition rather than a bare flash of the
  old page; (2) something like Alpine's x-cloak for transcript entries -
  right now, entries that are about to be hidden by a toggle (Show tool
  outputs/Show tool calls) can flash visible before the hide-tool-details/
  hide-tool-calls CSS rule (see session.php's own <style> block) actually
  applies, instead of never being visible at all.

Exploratory / speculative / lower priority:

- A search function to search within the contents of all sessions (from
  the dashboard), and within a single session (from the session sidebar) -
  broader than the plain name/title filter field the archived list
  already has.
- Research: investigate whether subagent calls (Agent/Task tool invocations) are
  retrievable from a session's own JSONL transcript, or only exist in a separate
  sub-transcript file - and more generally, survey what other info sits in these
  JSONL files that this app doesn't currently surface (sidechain/isSidechain
  entries, agent_id/agent_type fields, file-history-snapshot diffs, etc.) that
  might be worth rendering on session.php.
- Research: use reptyr to close the "no tmux pane at all" gap in the item just
  above, instead of leaving it a permanent residual "unmanageable" category.
  reptyr hijacks a running process's controlling terminal via ptrace and moves
  it to a new pty without killing/restarting it - so a bare claude process
  started in a plain terminal/SSH session (no tmux) could be adopted into a
  fresh tracked tmux pane after the fact, then picked up by the generalized
  SessionStart-hook sidecar registration from the item above like any other
  tmux-paned session. Andres's own starting-point sketch (needs review/
  hardening before use, not vetted yet - e.g. the sudo reptyr requirement,
  ptrace_scope implications, and whether this is safe to expose as a one-click
  dashboard action versus a manual host-side escape hatch):
    #!/bin/bash
    if [ "$1" != "--pid" ] || [ -z "$2" ]; then
        echo "Usage: tmuxify --pid <PID>"
        exit 1
    fi
    PID=$2
    if ! kill -0 "$PID" 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "Error: Process ID $PID does not exist."
        exit 1
    fi
    PROC_NAME=$(ps -p "$PID" -o comm= | tr -d '[:space:]')
    SESSION_NAME="tmuxify_${PROC_NAME}_${PID}"
    echo "Targeting process: $PROC_NAME (PID: $PID)"
    # Stop/background the process, then sever shell ownership so it survives
    # this terminal exiting (TSTP+CONT simulates Ctrl+Z then 'bg').
    kill -TSTP "$PID" 2>/dev/null
    kill -CONT "$PID" 2>/dev/null
    disown "$PID" 2>/dev/null
    tmux new-session -d -s "$SESSION_NAME"
    # sudo is used here because ptrace restrictions usually block non-root hijacking.
    tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION_NAME" "sudo reptyr $PID" C-m
    echo "Success! Process $PID moved to tmux session: $SESSION_NAME"
    sleep 1
    exec tmux attach-session -t "$SESSION_NAME"
- Future end-user/contributor separation concerns: remove hardcoded paths,
  README is out of date/too long (split into files if necessary).
